 full append-only audit history (`d6_containment_events`) read and attached, EVERY stop episode in that history enumerated with its exhaustion status taken from the history — the `prevEpisode*` summary alone is insufficient on its face, because it holds only the most recent closed episode and is overwritten by later clears. If any episode in the history is exhausted, the restoration must additionally name the R3-V authorization (§3.2) under which any later stop/verify work would proceed.

**What Julie is deciding, when the C3 decision eventually comes to her:** to deliberately enter a stopped state whose exit is a *separate future decision* with its own review, census, and GO. The stop act itself is irreversible by any operator act inside C3 — a deleted registration cannot be un-deleted; that irreversibility-without-restoration is the gate's design (it is the point of a stop), and it is why the stop's precondition (the armedAt expectation) is census-hard.

